            {'h': 'What business takes from open sources', 'p': 'OSINT is the collection and analysis of information that needs no separate permit. For a company it is a working way to check a partner, prepare for negotiations, look at a market, assess reputational risk or search public information on assets. Registers, courts, tenders, the media, filings, site archives and domain data are different pieces of one picture.'},
            {'h': 'Why the search itself is not a conclusion', 'p': 'There is a lot of open data, and it contains errors, stale records and deliberate confusion. Value appears when you select what answers the client’s question instead of listing everything found. Comparing sources, building a chronology and mapping links makes repeated addresses, shared directors and simultaneous changes of ownership visible.'},
            {'h': 'How a fact is separated from an assumption', 'p': 'An important fact is confirmed by at least two independent sources. The report keeps what was established separate from assumptions and from what the data does not allow you to assert. That protects the client from a decision built on one screenshot or an old news item.'},
