            {'h': 'What counts as commercial information', 'p': 'Client lists, prices, contract terms, technology, development plans and supplier data. The loss is rarely noticed on the day of the leak. More often the company sees the consequence. The first step is not to “protect everything” but to list what would hurt most and to name an owner for each category.'},
            {'h': 'Why a leak more often comes through people', 'p': 'In this picture most leaks are tied to excessive access, missing rules, personal devices and messengers, not to a complex outside attack. If an employee can see data the job does not need, the risk is already higher. Least privilege limits that: rights by role, a review after a change of post, access closed on the day someone leaves, and a log of critical data.'},
            {'h': 'Why paper still matters when you have passwords', 'p': 'A trade-secret regulation, NDAs and contract clauses do not replace technical access. They record what must not be disclosed and make a breach provable. Without them it is much harder to defend the right even when the team can already see that a leak happened.'},
            {'h': 'Rules people actually read', 'p': 'Technical and legal measures work when employees understand why they are there. Short regular briefings beat a thick policy nobody opens. If a leak has already happened, the sequence is different: record the facts, stop the spread and find the source. That mode is better written down in advance.'},
            {'h': 'What goes on the list of critical data', 'p': 'The list usually includes the client base, the price grid, the terms of framework contracts, a description of the technology, a plan to enter a new market and the contacts of key suppliers. Next to each category, name an owner inside the company. Without an owner, access spreads and later nobody knows who was supposed to see the file.'},
            {'h': 'Access that is reviewed after a staffing change', 'p': 'Role-based rights go stale on the day of a promotion, a transfer or a departure. Review them at those points, not “when there is time”. An access log is not there for total control. It shows, after an incident, who opened the material. Closing access on the day someone leaves shuts the shortest and most common window.'},
            {'p': 'Protection of commercial information rests on three supports already named: a short list of what is truly expensive to lose; minimal access and a review of it; documents that record the secret, plus short rules the team understands. If a leak already exists, the order is different: record, stop the spread, find the source.'},
